{
  "dom_id": "step-e14663",
  "name": "Example: Using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm",
  "problem": "Find the solutions of $50x \\equiv 120 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:298).$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\text{gcd}(50,298) =2.$*",
  "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=50$ and $n=298$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(50,298)=2.\n$$\n\nSince $b=120$ is divisible by $2,$ our congruence has $2$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50x & ≡120 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,298) \\\\ 2\\cdot 25x & ≡2\\cdot 60 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 149) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 25x & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 60 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 149) \\\\ 25x & ≡60 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have $a_1=25$ and $n_1=149$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(25,149)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $149.$\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149).$ To find the inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149),$ we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}149 & = & 25\\cdot 5 & + & 24\\, & ⇔ & \\,24 & = & 149-25\\cdot 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 25 & = & 24\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\, & ⇔ & \\,1 & = & 25-24\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 24 & = & 24\\cdot 1 & + & 0\\, &  & \\, &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1  = 6\\cdot 25 -149\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $149,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 25-149 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149) \\\\ 6\\cdot 25 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $25$ modulo $149$ is $6.$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $6,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25x & ≡60 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149) \\\\ 6\\cdot 25x & ≡6\\cdot 60 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149) \\\\ x & ≡360 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149) \\\\ x & ≡149\\cdot 2+62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149) \\\\ x & ≡62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,149)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x \\equiv 62 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:149).$\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $298$ (not modulo $149$). To find all the solutions modulo $298,$ we repeatedly add $149$ to $62$ modulo $298$ until we reach the number $62$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,298) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡62+149≡211 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,298) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡211+149≡360≡149\\cdot 2+62≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,298)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx_{1}=62\\: (\\text{mod}\\:298) \\quad\\text{ and }\\quad x_{2}= 211 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:298).\n$$",
